58 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 41 0 R
/E (As we drag ourselves to davening for Shvi'i Shel Pesach, we face a fascinating halachic reality. Unlike Shmini Atzeres, we do not recite Shehecheyanu by Kiddush tonight. The Gemara in Eruvin 40b and Pesachim 102b makes it clear that this day is not a regel bifnei atzmo; it is simply a continuation of the first day. At this point in the chag, surviving on a heavy diet of shmurah matzah and endless bowls of potato compote, the idea of functioning without a fresh spiritual recharge seems absolutely impossible. We desperately want a new brachah, a jolt of spiritual espresso to finally wake us up, but halachah says no. We are expected to completely coast on the original adrenaline of yetzias Mitzrayim. We have to find the immense strength to cross the Yam Suf using the exact same emunah we packed in our bags a week ago, without any new ceremonial fanfare. This profound lack of a new brachah perfectly explains the physically exhausting minhag brought down by the Mishnah Berurah 490:17. We are told to actively stay awake the entire night of Shvi'i Shel Pesach engaged in limud HaTorah, culminating in the loud recitation of Shiras HaYam at the break of dawn. Imagine the scene: we are physically depleted, running on fumes, yet we stay up all night learning Torah like we are back in kollel during the days of Elul zman. Why? Because staying up all night is the ultimate proof that we do not need a new Shehecheyanu to access the kedushah. We generate the energy ourselves through sheer limud HaTorah and tremendous mesiras nefesh. When we finally stand up at neitz to sing Az Yashir, we are demonstrating that true bitachon is not about waiting for a new flash of inspiration from Shamayim. It is about actively churning the spiritual waters ourselves, stubbornly keeping our heavy eyes open and our sefarim open, even when every fiber of our physical being is begging us to go back to sleep. The practical takeaway for our tzibbur is deeply relevant to our daily avodas Hashem. We constantly look for the next emotional high or the next magical Shehecheyanu moment to keep our davening and limud feeling fresh and exciting. But Shvi'i Shel Pesach teaches us that the real test of a true ben Torah is what happens during the long, quiet stretch when the initial excitement naturally fades. When you find yourself zoning out during a long Shacharis, fighting off sleep during night seder, or simply trying to survive the final exhausting days of Yom Tov without losing your temper at the Shabbos table, remember this exact night. You do not always get a fresh brachah to miraculously wake you up. Sometimes, you just have to firmly grab a shtender, crack open a sefer, and fiercely push through the overwhelming darkness until you finally see the waters split.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
