81 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 42 0 R
/E (As we exhaustedly collapse into Acharon Shel Pesach, we face a glaring halachic vacuum. Think about the first night of Yom Tov. We were armed with a massive arsenal of mitzvos maasiyos. We had shmurah matzah, bitter maror, four heavy kosos of wine, and a Haggadah that we read until our eyes literally glazed over. But today, on the eighth day, we have absolutely no unique physical mitzvos. The Sifsei Chaim in Moadim Volume 2 points out that this final day is completely devoid of physical props. We are left with only the general chiyuv of simchas Yom Tov. Let us be brutally honest: achieving simchah at this point, when our digestive systems are staging a violent protest against another piece of matzah and we have been trapped indoors with our hyperactive children for over a week, requires a literal miracle. Yet, this lack of physical mitzvos is precisely the point. Hashem is weaning us off the physical triggers of yetzias Mitzrayim. We do not need to chew on horseradish to feel close to Him anymore. We are expected to maintain our spiritual elevation purely through our internal emunah, without needing a specialized kezeiyas of crunchy carbohydrates to remind us who we are. This shift from physical props to pure, unadulterated connection flawlessly introduces our minhag to read Shir HaShirim during Pesach. The Abudraham in Seder Tefillos Pesach explains that we read it now because the Megillah vividly describes the springtime of our national redemption. Rabbi Akiva famously declares in Yadayim 3:5 that while all of Tanach is holy, Shir HaShirim is the absolute kodesh kodashim. It is a poetic, intense dialogue of raw love between Hashem and Klal Yisroel. When you connect this to our lack of physical mitzvos today, the message is incredibly powerful. On the first night, Hashem had to perform massive plagues, split a sea, and force-feed us matzah just to get our attention. But by Acharon Shel Pesach, we have finally matured. We have transitioned from needing dramatic miracles and heavy knaidlach to simply existing in a state of pure, loving connection. We read Shir HaShirim today because we have reached a level where our relationship with the Ribbono Shel Olam is based on deep, internal love rather than flashy, external mitzvos maasiyos. The practical takeaway for our tzibbur as we prepare to pack up our Pesach kitchens is to carry this internal Shir HaShirim into the mundane week ahead. You will not have matzah or Yom Tov meals to magically make you feel like a ben Torah tomorrow morning. When you are stuck in brutal morning traffic on your way to work, staring blankly at the bumper of a minivan, there are no physical mitzvos to instantly elevate your mood. You must rely on the internal, prop-free emunah you solidified today. Take the raw love of Acharon Shel Pesach and let it sustain your davening and middos long after the last box of dry, tasteless holiday cereal is violently shoved to the back of your pantry.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
